 
1727 Problem:-
You are given a binary matrix matrix of size m x n, and you are allowed to rearrange the columns of the matrix in any order.
Return the area of the largest submatrix within matrix where every element of the submatrix is 1 after reordering the columns optimally.

Example 1:
Input: matrix = [[0,0,1],[1,1,1],[1,0,1]]
Output: 4
Explanation: You can rearrange the columns as shown above.
The largest submatrix of 1s, in bold, has an area of 4.

Example 2:
Input: matrix = [[1,0,1,0,1]]
Output: 3
Explanation: You can rearrange the columns as shown above.
The largest submatrix of 1s, in bold, has an area of 3.

Example 3:
Input: matrix = [[1,1,0],[1,0,1]]
Output: 2
Explanation: Notice that you must rearrange entire columns, and there is no way to make a submatrix of 1s larger than an area of 2.
 
Constraints:
m == matrix.length
n == matrix[i].length
1 <= m * n <= 105
matrix[i][j] is either 0 or 1.

=====================================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-
Intuition
A submatrix consisting entirely of 1's can be viewed column-wise as heights of consecutive ones ending at the current row.
If we know the height of each column at a given row, the problem becomes similar to finding the Largest Rectangle in Histogram.
However, this problem allows rearranging columns independently for every row.

Approach
Hence, we can reorder the column heights to place taller columns together, maximizing the possible rectangle width.
Thus, for every row:
We treat each value as the height of consecutive 1's ending at that row.
Rearrange (sort) these heights.
If the j−th column in this sorted order has height h, we can form a rectangle with:
height=h
width=j+1
because the first j+1 columns all have height≥h.
The Area becomes:
Area=h×(j+1)
Then, we compute the maximum across all rows.

Algorithm:-
Build vertical heights
Traverse the matrix row by row.
If matrix[i][j]=1, add the height from the cell above:
matrix[i][j]+=matrix[i−1][j]
This converts the matrix into heights of consecutive 1's ending at each row.
Rearrange columns per row
For each row, sort the heights.
Sorting simulates the optimal column rearrangement.
sorted in descending order for simplicity.

Compute maximum area
For each position j in the sorted row:
Treat matrix[i][j] as the rectangle height.
The width is j+1.

Update:
Area=height×width
Track the global maximum area across all rows.

Time complexity: O(m×nlogn)
Space complexity: O(1)

COde:-
class Solution {
    public int largestSubmatrix(int[][] matrix) {
        int m = matrix.length, n = matrix[0].length;
        int res = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
                if (matrix[i][j] == 1)
                    matrix[i][j] += matrix[i - 1][j];

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            Arrays.sort(matrix[i]);
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
                res = Math.max(res, matrix[i][j] * (n - j));
        }

        return res;
    }
}
